# Verification before completion

## The rule

An assertion is a claim. Evidence is the command output that supports it. Report
the evidence or withdraw the claim.

The failure mode is not dishonesty, it is fluency: having written the fix, the
sentence "tests pass" is simply the next sentence, and it feels true because the
code looks right. It is cheap to be wrong here and expensive to be wrong in a
pull request.

## Before saying "done", run it

For each claim you are about to make, run the command that would falsify it and
read the output to the end.

| You are about to claim | Run |
| --- | --- |
| Tests pass | The full suite, not one file, and not only the tests you touched |
| It builds | The real build command, after the change, with the config that ships |
| It compiles | The type checker and the linter, which are separate programs with separate exit codes |
| Nothing else broke | The suite, plus any caller you changed the signature of |
| The file was created | List the directory. The write may have gone elsewhere. |
| The fix works | The exact failing command from the bug report, re-run |

An exit code of zero is necessary and not sufficient. Read the output: suites
that report skipped tests, and reporters that summarise failures as warnings,
both exit zero.

## Verify the negatives deliberately

Positive claims are easy to check. Negative claims are where the real errors
live, because nothing fails when you skip them.

Before saying a change is safe, actively look for the breakage:

```sh
# Who else calls the function whose signature changed?
grep -rn "processPayment(" --include=*.ts src/

# Is the thing I removed actually unreferenced?
grep -rn "legacyExport" --include=*.ts src/
```

Then check the surfaces that have no test: migrations against an existing
database, the path taken only in production configuration, the branch that only
runs when an environment variable is set.

## Verify the claim, not the intention

Distinguish:

- "I changed `handler.ts`" — verified by reading the file you changed.
- "The request handler now validates the payload" — verified by a test that
  fails without the validation.
- "This fixes the reported bug" — verified by re-running the reported command.

The third is the claim users care about, and it is the one most often asserted
without a re-run.

## If verification fails

Say so immediately and specifically: the command, the output, and what you think
is still wrong. A partial result reported honestly is more useful than a
complete one reported wrongly, and the user can act on the first.

Do not soften a failure into a question, and do not report a known-failing
suite as passing because the failing test is unrelated to your change. It is
still failing, and the person relying on your report does not know that.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "I just ran it a moment ago." | Before the last edit. Run it after. |
| "It obviously works, I wrote it." | The test suite is what the user's environment will believe, not your confidence. |
| "The failing test is unrelated." | If so, say so explicitly. Do not let it ride in silence. |
| "I will check it after committing." | After committing is after the claim has been made. |
| "The output looked fine." | Read it to the end. Failures often appear before the summary. |
| "Running the full suite takes too long." | Then run the subset you touched and say that is what you ran. |
